# Trivy suppressions for the CI image scans (Issues #131, #226).
#
# One file backs all four scans — finance-app, finance-migrate, finance-importer
# and finance-backup — so an entry here silences its CVE in EVERY image, not only
# the one that reported it. That is a deliberate simplification while the file is
# short: it keeps the single reviewable authority CONTRIBUTING.md links to. If
# entries start accumulating, split it into .trivy/<image>.trivyignore and point
# each scan step's `trivyignores:` at its own file, rather than letting one
# image's suppression widen to three that never needed it.
#
# The CI gate blocks on HIGH and CRITICAL findings that have a fix available
# (`ignore-unfixed: true`), so an entry here means "we know, and we have
# decided to ship anyway". That decision needs to be reviewable, so every
# entry must carry:
#
#   1. the CVE / advisory ID
#   2. why it does not apply, or why the fix cannot be taken yet
#   3. an expiry date — Trivy honours `exp:` and the finding returns after it
#
# Format:
#   CVE-YYYY-NNNNN exp:YYYY-MM-DD
#   # <why this is suppressed, and what unblocks removing it>
#
# Prefer rebuilding on a patched base image over adding an entry. If a
# suppression outlives two expiry extensions, that is a signal to change the
# base image rather than extend it a third time.
#
# Two of the four bases are not freely bumpable, which is worth knowing before
# reaching for an entry. postgres:18.6 (finance-backup) must stay version-matched
# to the server, so moving it means moving docker-compose.yml and the workflow
# service containers with it. Both it and python:3.14-slim (finance-importer) are
# rebuilt upstream under the same tag, so for an OS-package finding on either,
# re-running the job after upstream rebuilds is frequently the entire fix — check
# that before suppressing anything.
#
# Populate only from real findings, never pre-emptively.
#
# The entries below were seeded from the first scan of all four images (#226),
# which is the run that turned three previously unscanned images into gated ones.
# Every one of them is a base-image or bundled-tooling finding — none comes from
# code or a dependency this repo declares — and every one is scheduled for review
# on the same date, because the thing that clears most of them is the same event:
# an upstream rebuild of the base image.
#
# A second generation joined them in #291 and has since been pruned in #303,
# which is the first time this file has gone backwards and is worth recording as
# the normal end of an entry's life: the reporter named eight entries as matching
# nothing, and they were deleted rather than left to expire. The widening this
# file's opening note warns about was concrete while they were here — the Debian
# util-linux entries silenced those CVEs on the two Alpine images too, which
# never reported them — and it was concrete again, harmlessly, in the OpenSSL
# entry that sat below until 2026-10-03: that one was reported by all four. The
# three debian entries that replaced it have since gone the same way (see the
# section that held them, below). Every entry left is npm's vendored tooling,
# which only finance-migrate reports, so each one widens to three images that
# never reported it — harmless for exactly as long as those three keep not
# reporting it.
#
# NOTHING REMOVES AN ENTRY WHEN THE BASE IS REBUILT. The CVE simply stops being
# reported and the line stays here, inert, until someone deletes it — `exp:` stops
# the suppression working, it does not delete anything.
# scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh runs at the end of the image job and names
# the entries that no longer match a finding in any image; those are the ones to
# remove, along with their comment.

# --- debian OS packages (finance-backup, finance-importer) -------------------
CVE-2026-109518 exp:2026-11-18
# apt 3.0.3 and libapt-pkg7.0 3.0.3, fixed in 3.0.4 (DSA-6607-1, in
# trixie-security). Reported by both debian images. NVD published it on
# 2026-11-04, after the last upstream rebuilds Docker Hub reports
# (python:3.14-slim 2026-11-02, postgres:18.6 2026-10-31), so both bases still
# carry 3.0.3 and only a rebuild clears it. The vulnerable path is not taken
# here: the advisory needs apt to *install* a package over HTTP that an on-path
# attacker, mirror or proxy can manipulate, with Acquire::Retries above zero.
# Neither image runs apt at all. importer/Dockerfile and scripts/Dockerfile have
# no apt-get step, and nothing at runtime (`python poll.py`, `pg_dump`) calls
# it. Dated weeks out, per the note below. Expect
# scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh to name it stale once both tags rebuild.
#
# EMPTIED ONCE ALREADY, BECAUSE THE BASE CAUGHT UP. Before the entry above,
# this block held three entries from #352,
# all debian 13.7 packages already fixed in -deb13u3 and waiting only on a base
# rebuild to carry it: CVE-2026-75804 and CVE-2026-84782 (OpenSSL
# 3.5.7-1~deb13u2: QUIC flow control, DTLS retransmission) and CVE-2026-103111
# (libpcre2-8-0 10.46-1~deb13u2, reported by finance-importer as well). Docker
# Hub reports postgres:18.6 rebuilt at 2026-10-06 07:10 UTC and python:3.14-slim
# at 08:11, and scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh named all three as matching
# nothing in any image on PR #360's run the next day. Deleted here with their
# comments on that CI verdict, as CVE-2026-14456 — the OpenSSL entry they had
# replaced — was one generation earlier.
#
# TWO THINGS WORTH KEEPING. The short dates were right both times: a fix debian
# has already published, waiting only on a base rebuild, clears in days or
# weeks, not a quarter, so date the next one weeks out rather than parking it
# with the 2026-11-17 block below. And read every scan's summary, not the one
# next to the error. These entries were first recorded as finance-backup's alone
# because only the tail of the job log was read: finance-backup is the last of
# the four scans, and finance-migrate and finance-importer had failed above it.

# --- python:3.14-slim bundled packages (finance-importer) -------------------
# EMPTY, AND THAT IS THE POINT. This block held CVE-2025-47273 (setuptools
# 70.3.0) and GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 (msgpack 1.1.2), both of them there because
# the image shipped pip. importer/Dockerfile now removes pip after the install
# layer — done to fix urllib3 CVE-2026-97687 / CVE-2026-97689, which pip
# vendored the same way and which no requirements.txt pin could reach — and
# scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh named both of these from the CI run on the
# very next push. Deleted here on that verdict, which is the normal end of an
# entry's life and the second time this file has gone backwards after #303.
#
# TWO THINGS WORTH KEEPING FROM HOW THAT WENT. The setuptools entry's own
# comment had already worked out the answer — "could be removed outright if the
# importer image ever drops pip after the install layer" — and it sat there for
# weeks while the entry was renewed instead. A suppression that names its own
# exit is a task, not a decision; read the comments before extending the dates.
#
# And the change that removed pip predicted only msgpack would clear, on the
# reasoning that setuptools was a top-level site-package left untouched. CI said
# both. The setuptools entry was right and that prediction was wrong, which is
# the same lesson in the opposite direction: this file's verdicts come from CI,
# including the ones that are better than expected.
#
# The #291 warning that lived here is the reason both entries survived to be
# deleted properly rather than guessed away, so it is kept: a local run of
# scripts/check-trivy-suppressions.sh once reported these two stale and a local
# `ls site-packages` appeared to confirm it. Both were wrong — the machine's
# cached python:3.14-slim was three weeks older than the one CI pulls, and the
# packages lived under pip/_vendor/, not at the top level. CI failed on the very
# next push. Trust a staleness verdict from CI, where the image was just built
# from a freshly pulled base — not from a workstation.

# --- npm's own vendored dependencies (finance-migrate) ----------------------
# The migrate stage deliberately keeps npm — it runs `npx drizzle-kit` — where
# the runner stage deletes it precisely because of findings like these. They live
# in /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm/node_modules/ and are fixed only when
# node:24-alpine ships a newer bundled npm; no application-level bump reaches
# them, and `npm update` inside the image would defeat the pinned build.
CVE-2026-102276 exp:2026-11-17
# brace-expansion 5.0.9, fixed in 5.0.10. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-102278 exp:2026-11-17
# brace-expansion 5.0.9, fixed in 5.0.11. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-19534 exp:2026-11-17
# undici 6.27.0, fixed in 6.28.1. npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-109383 exp:2026-11-17
# undici 6.28.0, fixed in 6.29.1 (GHSA-2m5v-pch6-74hx, TLS certificate
# validation bypass via dropped tls options). npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-109869 exp:2026-11-17
# undici 6.28.0, fixed in 6.29.1 (GHSA-xjj6-f33q-929c, denial of service via
# an unhandled error on excessive content-encodings). npm-vendored, as above.
CVE-2026-87018 exp:2026-11-17
# undici 6.28.0, fixed in 6.29.1 (GHSA-qrxm-fqhp-jwvm, denial of service via
# TLS 1.2 renegotiation with a mismatched certificate). npm-vendored, as above.
#
# Trivy now reads the vendored copy as 6.28.0 rather than 6.27.0. That still
# sits below CVE-2026-19534's 6.28.1, so that entry keeps matching. The first
# two IDs were also reported against the application tree's undici 7.30.0 and
# jsdom's 8.11.2. Those are lockfile copies, so they were bumped (7.30.1,
# 8.12.2) and are not what these lines cover. Because an ID entry widens, these
# would also hide a lockfile regression from this scan. The npm audit gate
# still catches that on the GHSAs.
#
# All three arrived together, and the first attempt listed only one. Trivy
# prints several CVEs against one installed version as a single row, with the
# version cell merged, so reading only the rows that name a version undercounts.
# Count the IDs under the scan's "Total:" line, not the version cells.
#
# THE 2026-10-03 THREE (CVE-2026-102276, CVE-2026-102278 AND CVE-2026-19534)
# ARRIVED WITH A CLEAN DEMONSTRATION OF WHY THIS BLOCK IS SCOPED
# TO VENDORED COPIES, which is worth recording because it is the distinction the
# block is easiest to get wrong about. finance-migrate reported 15 HIGH in
# node-pkg on 2026-10-03: this image carries app/node_modules so that the
# migrate stage can run `npx drizzle-kit`, so the scan sees the application's
# dependency tree AND npm's vendored one, with the same package names appearing
# at different versions in each. An `npm update` of undici and brace-expansion
# in app/package-lock.json cleared twelve of the fifteen and needed no entry
# here. The three left are the vendored copies at the versions this block's
# note already named as npm 11.19.0's bundle — brace-expansion 5.0.7 and undici
# 6.27.0 — which no application-level bump can reach. If a finding here names a
# version that matches app/package-lock.json, it belongs in the lockfile, not in
# this file.
#
# The installed versions above are the point of this block after #303, and they
# are why partial clears are the normal case rather than a curiosity. This
# section held eight entries; node:24-alpine shipped npm 11.19.0, which moved
# tar 7.5.16 → 7.5.19, brace-expansion 5.0.6 → 5.0.7 and undici 6.26.0 → 6.27.0,
# and exactly the four entries whose fix version that reached went dead. Those
# four survived because their fix was still ahead of what npm bundles, and the
# three added above bring the block to seven. A vendored bump therefore clears
# *some* of a package's entries and leaves others, so re-read the installed
# version here before assuming a comment is still true — brace-expansion and
# undici now carry two generations of advisory each, against one installed
# version apiece.
#
# IT HAPPENED AGAIN BY 2026-11-05, in both directions at once. node:24-alpine
# has been rebuilt since this block was last pruned (Docker Hub's latest is
# 2026-10-28), and its bundled npm moved the vendored copies far enough to kill
# four entries. PR #382's run named them as matching nothing in any image, and
# they are deleted here with their comments on that verdict:
#   CVE-2026-14257  brace-expansion, fixed in 5.0.8
#   CVE-2026-69152  brace-expansion, fixed in 5.0.9
#   CVE-2026-69192  ip-address, fixed in 10.3.1
#   CVE-2026-73566  tar, fixed in 7.5.21 (#291)
# Over the same stretch undici moved only 6.27.0 -> 6.28.0, which cleared
# nothing and left it exposed to the three 6.29.1 advisories above. brace-expansion's version
# is inferred rather than read: 5.0.9's fix went dead while 5.0.10's still
# matches, so the vendored copy is 5.0.9, and the two comments say so. The
# block is now six entries, against two installed versions.

# Not listed here, and excluded at the scanner instead: the Go stdlib CVEs
# reported against drizzle-kit's vendored `esbuild` and the postgres image's
# `gosu`. Both are stripped third-party binaries this repo neither compiles nor
# can patch, so they are `skip-files` entries on their scan steps in ci.yml with
# the reasoning recorded there. Suppressing them by ID would mean a new entry
# every time the Go project publishes a stdlib advisory, forever.
